          "type": "intro",
          "title": "The Defense Rand Called a Surrender",
          "content": "Picture America in the mid-1960s. The economy is booming, the Cold War is at full heat, and plenty of people are happy to defend capitalism. Their argument is almost always the same: **it works**. It produces more cars, more food, more television sets than the Soviet system.\n\nAyn Rand thought that argument was a disaster. In **Capitalism: The Unknown Ideal** (1966), she claims the system is losing ground not because its critics are smarter, but because its friends never defended it on **moral** grounds. They apologized for profit, praised capitalism for serving \"society,\" and quietly agreed that self-interest was a little shameful.\n\nOver the next 12 lessons you will meet her essays, her collaborators, her enemies on the left AND the right, and the economists and historians who answer back. First question: what exactly does she mean by an \"unknown\" ideal?",
