        {
          "type": "concept",
          "title": "Three Theories of \"the Good\"",
          "content": "One of the most original moves in \"What Is Capitalism?\" is Rand's claim that every political system rests on a theory of what \"good\" means. She sorts them into three:\n\n• **Intrinsic** -- good is a property of things or actions in themselves, regardless of anyone's judgment or benefit. If the good is simply \"out there,\" whoever claims to see it can impose it on others. Rand links this to theocracy and dictatorship.\n• **Subjective** -- good is whatever someone feels or wants. With no standard, disputes are settled by whoever has the most power. She links this to pressure-group politics.\n• **Objective** -- good is an evaluation of reality by a rational mind, according to a standard of value (a human life). Because it must be grasped by each person's own reason, it can only be spread by **persuasion**, not force.\n\nHer conclusion: capitalism is the only system consistent with the objective theory, because it leaves each person free to judge and choose. Critics find the three-way sort tidy but question whether real ethical theories fit so neatly.",
          "visual": {
            "type": "diagram",
            "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">THREE THEORIES</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"23\">What does good mean?</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"180\" width=\"300\" height=\"420\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"230\" y=\"245\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">INTRINSIC</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"300\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Good in itself</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"340\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">regardless of anyone</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"420\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">Leads to: imposed</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"450\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">by those who see it</text><rect x=\"400\" y=\"180\" width=\"300\" height=\"420\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.12)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"245\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">SUBJECTIVE</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"300\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Good = whatever</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"340\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">someone wants</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"420\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">Leads to: rule by</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"450\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">the strongest group</text><rect x=\"720\" y=\"180\" width=\"300\" height=\"420\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"870\" y=\"245\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">OBJECTIVE</text><text x=\"870\" y=\"300\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Judged by reason</text><text x=\"870\" y=\"340\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">against a standard</text><text x=\"870\" y=\"420\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">Leads to: freedom</text><text x=\"870\" y=\"450\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">and persuasion</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"680\" width=\"800\" height=\"110\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"745\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-size=\"22\">Rand: only capitalism fits the objective view</text></svg>",
            "caption": "Rand's three theories of the good"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "title": "The Trader Principle",
          "content": "If rights mark the boundaries, the **trader principle** describes how people should deal with each other inside them. Rand introduced it in \"The Objectivist Ethics\" (The Virtue of Selfishness) and it runs through this book.\n\nThe idea: in every relationship -- business, friendship, even love -- people should deal as **traders**, exchanging **value for value** by free, mutual consent. Neither side sacrifices itself to the other, and neither side seizes what it has not earned.\n\n• A trader does not beg for the unearned (the moocher).\n• A trader does not take by force (the looter).\n• A trader offers something the other person wants in return.\n\nIn the market, prices emerge from millions of such free judgments. Rand calls market value **socially objective** -- not the \"true\" philosophical value of a thing, but the sum of what free individuals judge it to be worth. A great novel may sell less than a trashy one; she accepts that as the price of freedom.\n\nCritics ask whether love or parenting really fit a trade model. Rand replied that she meant spiritual values too -- you love someone for the values they embody.",
          "visual": {
            "type": "diagram",
            "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">THE TRADER PRINCIPLE</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"23\">Value for value, by consent</text><circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"330\" r=\"110\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.3)\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"250\" y=\"325\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3b82f6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">TRADER A</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"360\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">offers value</text><circle cx=\"850\" cy=\"330\" r=\"110\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.3)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"850\" y=\"325\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">TRADER B</text><text x=\"850\" y=\"360\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">offers value</text><path d=\"M370 300 L720 300\" stroke=\"#14b8a6\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><polygon points=\"730,300 716,291 716,309\" fill=\"#14b8a6\"/><path d=\"M730 360 L380 360\" stroke=\"#14b8a6\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><polygon points=\"370,360 384,351 384,369\" fill=\"#14b8a6\"/><rect x=\"80\" y=\"540\" width=\"440\" height=\"200\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"605\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">NOT THE MOOCHER</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"655\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Begs for the unearned</text><rect x=\"580\" y=\"540\" width=\"440\" height=\"200\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"605\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">NOT THE LOOTER</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"655\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Takes by force</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"810\" width=\"800\" height=\"110\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"875\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-size=\"22\">Both sides gain; neither is sacrificed</text></svg>",
            "caption": "Voluntary exchange as a moral ideal"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "The \"Common Good\" Trap",
          "content": "Rand's most pointed criticism of capitalism's usual defenders targets the phrase **\"the common good.\"** Economists, she says, often justified free markets because they produce the greatest good for the greatest number. That sounds friendly to capitalism. She thought it was a Trojan horse.\n\nHer reasoning: if the justification for your freedom is that it benefits society, then the moment someone argues society would benefit more from controlling you, your freedom has no defense left. The individual becomes a means to a collective end -- what she called the **tribal premise**.\n\nConsider a concrete kind of case she had in mind: a city takes a family's land by **eminent domain** for a project officials say serves the public. On a common-good standard, the only question is whether the project is beneficial enough. On Rand's rights standard, the question is whether the family consented.\n\nCritics reply that every society must make some collective decisions (roads, defense) and that \"rights\" themselves are usually defended partly by their good consequences. Rand's answer: consequences matter, but they cannot be the moral foundation.",
          "visual": {
            "type": "diagram",
            "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">THE COMMON GOOD TRAP</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"23\">Same case, two standards</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"170\" width=\"800\" height=\"120\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(255,255,255,0.05)\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"225\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">A city wants a family s land for a project</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"262\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">The family does not want to sell</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"360\" width=\"460\" height=\"300\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.12)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"420\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">COMMON GOOD</text><text x=\"290\" y=\"475\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Does the public benefit</text><text x=\"290\" y=\"510\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">outweigh the family?</text><text x=\"290\" y=\"580\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">Answer depends on officials</text><rect x=\"580\" y=\"360\" width=\"460\" height=\"300\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"810\" y=\"420\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">RAND S RIGHTS</text><text x=\"810\" y=\"475\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Did the owners consent?</text><text x=\"810\" y=\"510\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">If not, it is a seizure</text><text x=\"810\" y=\"580\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">Answer depends on consent</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"730\" width=\"800\" height=\"120\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"785\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-size=\"21\">Rand: the tribal premise treats</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"822\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-size=\"21\">the individual as a means</text></svg>",
            "caption": "Why Rand distrusted the phrase \"the common good\""
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "quote",
          "content": "Capitalism is a social system based on the recognition of individual rights, including property rights, in which all property is privately owned.",
          "author": "Ayn Rand, \"What Is Capitalism?\" in Capitalism: The Unknown Ideal (1966)",
          "visual": {
            "type": "diagram",
            "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">IN HER WORDS</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"23\">The definition itself</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"220\" width=\"900\" height=\"360\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"300\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-style=\"italic\" font-size=\"24\">Capitalism is a social system based on</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"350\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-style=\"italic\" font-size=\"24\">the recognition of individual rights,</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-style=\"italic\" font-size=\"24\">including property rights, in which</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"450\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-style=\"italic\" font-size=\"24\">all property is privately owned.</text><text x=\"900\" y=\"530\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-size=\"20\">-- Ayn Rand, 1966</text><rect x=\"200\" y=\"680\" width=\"700\" height=\"100\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"740\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-size=\"21\">Note what is missing: no mention of GDP</text></svg>",
            "caption": "Rand's definition of capitalism"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "title": "Where Economists and Philosophers Push Back",
          "content": "Rand's definition is clear, but critics raise several serious objections. (These are the critics' views; weigh them yourself.)\n\n• **A stipulated ideal.** Economists usually define capitalism descriptively: private ownership of capital, markets, wage labor. Rand defines it as a perfect moral ideal, which means no real economy ever qualifies. Critics say that makes the theory hard to test -- every failure belongs to \"the mixed economy.\"\n• **Market failures.** Mainstream economics identifies cases where free exchange alone underperforms: **public goods** (like national defense, which even she assigns to government), **externalities** like pollution, and information problems. Rand usually answers that these arise from undefined property rights.\n• **Deriving rights.** Philosopher **Robert Nozick** (1971) argued that Rand's step from \"my life is my highest value\" to \"I must respect everyone else's rights\" has gaps.\n• **Funding government.** Rand opposed taxation as coercive and floated voluntary financing, which even sympathetic readers find hard to picture working.\n\nDefenders reply that a moral ideal is supposed to be a standard to aim at, not a description of current reality.",
          "visual": {
            "type": "diagram",
            "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">THE PUSHBACK</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"23\">Four serious objections</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"180\" width=\"440\" height=\"200\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"245\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">STIPULATED IDEAL</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"295\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">No real economy qualifies</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"330\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">Hard to test</text><rect x=\"580\" y=\"180\" width=\"440\" height=\"200\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.12)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"245\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">MARKET FAILURES</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"295\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Public goods, pollution</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"330\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">Rand: undefined property</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"420\" width=\"440\" height=\"200\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"485\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">NOZICK, 1971</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"535\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Gaps from egoism to rights</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"570\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">On the Randian Argument</text><rect x=\"580\" y=\"420\" width=\"440\" height=\"200\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.15)\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"485\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3b82f6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">NO TAXES?</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"535\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Voluntary government funding</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"570\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">Hard to picture working</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"700\" width=\"800\" height=\"120\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"752\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"21\">Defenders: an ideal is a standard to aim at</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"790\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">not a description of today</text></svg>",
            "caption": "Main criticisms of Rand's definition"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "application",
          "title": "Steelman, Then Critique",
          "content": "Practice arguing about Rand's definition the way a good seminar would. Pick one policy -- say, a city banning short-term rentals, or a tax-funded public library.\n\n1. **State Rand's case in its strongest form.** Who owns the property? Did anyone use force or fraud? What would a strict separation of state and economics say?\n\n2. **Apply the three theories of the good.** Is the policy justified by an intrinsic good, by what a group wants, or by a reasoned standard each person could check?\n\n3. **Run the trader test.** Is every party dealing value for value by consent, or is someone paying for something they did not choose?\n\n4. **Now critique.** Is there an externality, a public good, or a fairness concern Rand's framework handles poorly?\n\n5. **Write one sentence** saying which argument you find stronger and why. Label it as your opinion.\n\nIf you can do step 1 well enough that a Rand admirer would nod, you understand the essay.",
          "visual": {
            "type": "diagram",
            "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">THE SEMINAR DRILL</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"23\">One policy, five steps</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"170\" width=\"800\" height=\"90\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"225\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">1.</text><text x=\"245\" y=\"225\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">Steelman Rand s rights case</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"290\" width=\"800\" height=\"90\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"345\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">2.</text><text x=\"245\" y=\"345\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">Apply the three theories of the good</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"410\" width=\"800\" height=\"90\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"465\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">3.</text><text x=\"245\" y=\"465\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">Run the trader test</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"530\" width=\"800\" height=\"90\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.12)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"585\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">4.</text><text x=\"245\" y=\"585\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">Critique: externality? public good?</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"650\" width=\"800\" height=\"90\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(236,72,153,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ec4899\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"705\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#ec4899\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">5.</text><text x=\"245\" y=\"705\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">Write your verdict as opinion</text></svg>",
            "caption": "Arguing with the definition fairly"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "quiz",
          "question": "Why does Rand reject defending capitalism on the grounds that it serves \"the common good\"?",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "Because she believed capitalism actually harms most people in the short run",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Because it makes freedom depend on a collective benefit, so freedom loses whenever controls promise more",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "Because the phrase was invented by Soviet propagandists and had no meaning in America",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Because she thought economists could not measure social benefits accurately",
              "correct": false
            }
          ],
          "explanation": "Rand argues that grounding freedom in the common good accepts the tribal premise: the individual is justified only by serving the group. Then anyone who claims a control would serve society better has already won the moral argument. She did think capitalism benefits people widely, but insisted that benefit is a consequence, not the justification. Her objection is moral, not about measurement.",
          "visual": {
            "type": "diagram",
            "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"34\">KNOWLEDGE CHECK</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"160\" width=\"800\" height=\"200\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.1)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">THE COMMON GOOD</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">A friend of capitalism -- or a trap?</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"320\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">Think about the tribal premise</text><rect x=\"120\" y=\"440\" width=\"400\" height=\"140\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.12)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"320\" y=\"520\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-size=\"22\">Benefit as proof?</text><rect x=\"580\" y=\"440\" width=\"400\" height=\"140\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"780\" y=\"520\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"22\">Rights as proof?</text></svg>",
            "caption": "Final check"
          }
        }
